{"episode_id": "0e369ff8ae2b", "role": "orchestrator", "agent_id": "orchestrator", "dispatch_id": null, "group_id": null, "arbitration_id": null, "step": 9, "call_id": "call-9", "name": "reply", "t_start": 1792141293.6729972, "t_end": 1792141293.673137, "exit_code": null, "timed_out": false, "is_error": false, "output_chars": 25, "tool_call_id": "call_resp_01a143f1-e3cc-770b-8005-50ff22d73e70_0", "arguments": {"done": true, "eta_min": 0, "text": "Fixed. In errors.go, `Unwrap()` was declared on `*Error` (pointer receiver), so a plain `Error` value — which is what you passed to `fmt.Errorf(\"%w\", ...)` — did not implement `interface{ Unwrap() error }`. The `%w` verb therefore stored no unwrappable error, and both `errors.Is` and `errors.As` reported \"not found\". Changed it to a value receiver (`func (msg Error) Unwrap() error`), matching the existing value-receiver `Error()` method, so both `Error` and `*Error` unwrap correctly. Verified: `errors.Is(wrapped, innerErr)` and `errors.As(wrapped, &target)` are now true for the value form, the pointer form still works, and the full `go test ./` suite passes.", "to": 1}}
